{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q11",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "LoRA trains under 1% of a model's parameters, cutting GPU memory and letting adapters be swapped per task \u2014 given that efficiency, is there ever a real quality reason to still pick full fine-tuning over it, and if so, what is it?",
  "answer": "Yes.  While LoRA\u2019s parameter\u2011efficiency makes it attractive, full fine\u2011tuning can still be the better choice when the target task demands a **substantially larger behavioural shift** than the base model already supports.  \n\n* Full fine\u2011tuning updates every weight, giving the model the **largest hypothesis space** and, in most published studies, a **small but real edge in final task performance** \u2013 especially on tasks that are **far outside the model\u2019s pre\u2011training distribution**\u202f\u3010full-fine-tuning-vs-adapters::0\u3011.  \n* That quality gap **widens** for tasks that require the model to acquire **new capabilities** that the frozen base cannot capture with a low\u2011rank adapter\u202f\u3010full-fine-tuning-vs-adapters::1\u3011.  \n* Consequently, practitioners \u201cescalate to full fine\u2011tuning only if a measured evaluation gap actually justifies the added memory and storage cost\u201d\u202f\u3010full-fine-tuning-vs-adapters::2\u3011.  \n\nSo the real quality reason to pick full fine\u2011tuning is when the task\u2019s demands are sufficiently different from what the frozen model can already do, and the resulting performance gain outweighs the extra computational and storage cost.",
